esented by matrices  each row of the Matrix will correspond  to a possible value of x each column  will correspond to a possible value of y  in the entry where they intersect will  be the output of the function on that  pair of inputs  the example shown here is the  communication Matrix for the equality  function the equality function which is  the main character of today's talk  evaluates to one if and only if the  inputs are equal  we can use properties of the equality  functions communication Matrix to reason  about its communication complexity  we know that functions with efficient  deterministic protocols have  communication matrices with low rank  the equality function's communication  Matrix has high rank  therefore we can conclude that there is  no efficient deterministic protocol for  equality  however there is an efficient randomized  protocol for equality that uses hashing  what other functions have efficient  randomized protocols  one such function is the greater than  function interpreting our inputs as  numbers is X greater than y  the fact that equality has an efficient  randomized protocol makes it easy to  show that the greater than function does  as well  We Run The equality protocol multiple  times to binary search for the first  coordinate I where X and Y differ  then Alice and Bob exchange x i and y i  if x i is greater than y i  then X is greater than y  we used a reduction here so this  randomized protocol can be described  equally well as a deterministic protocol  with access to an equality Oracle  certainly every function with an  efficient equality Oracle protocol also  has an efficient randomized protocol  this raises the question of the converse  does every function with an efficient  randomized protocol also have an  efficient equality Oracle protocol  chatapatia Lovett and venials answer  this in the negative  the goal of our paper is to continue  investigating the relationship between  equality oracles and Randomness  specifically in t